CHAPTER: POLYMORPHISM
1. Introduction
    What is Polymorphism?

        The word Polymorphism is derived from two Greek words.

        Poly  → Many

        Morph → Forms

        Hence,Polymorphism means "One name, many forms."

        In Java, the same method name can perform different tasks depending on the situation.

    Definition

        Polymorphism is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concept in which a single interface, method, or object can take many forms or exhibit different behaviors.

        Real-Life Examples
        Example 1: Person
        Teacher → teaches

        Doctor → treats patients

        Engineer → develops software

    All are persons but perform different work.

    Example 2: Animal

            Animal makes sound

            Dog  → Bark

            Cat  → Meow

            Cow  → Moo

            Same action

            sound()

    Different implementation.

    Example 3: Mobile Operating System

                AndroidOS

                ↓

                SamsungOS

                ↓

                OnePlusOS

                ↓

                MIOS

    Each mobile shows a different home screen.

    Why Polymorphism?

        Without polymorphism

        displayStudent()

        displayEmployee()

        displayTeacher()

        Too many methods.

        With polymorphism

    display()

    Same method name.

    Different behavior.

    This makes programs

    Cleaner
    Easier to understand
    Easier to maintain
    More reusable


Types of Polymorphism
                     Polymorphism
                          |
            ------------------------------
            |                            |
     Compile Time                  Runtime
    (Method Overloading)      (Method Overriding)


Method

Definition

    A method is a block of code that performs a specific task.

Example

    void display()
    {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }

Components

    Return Type

    Method Name

    Parameters

    Method Body

    METHOD OVERLOADING

    Definition

        Method Overloading means having multiple methods with the same name inside the same class but with different parameter lists.

Why do we use it?

    Suppose we have a Calculator.

Addition can be

    10 + 20

    10 + 20 + 30

    5.5 + 8.5

Instead of writing

    addTwoNumbers()

    addThreeNumbers()

    addDoubleNumbers()

    We simply write

    add()

Java decides which method to call.

    Rules of Method Overloading

        Methods must have

        ✓ Same name

        Different

        ✓ Number of parameters

        OR

        ✓ Type of parameters

        OR

        ✓ Order of parameters

        Return type alone cannot overload methods.

Examples:

    Valid
        display()

        display(int age)

        display(String name)

        display(int age, String name)


    Invalid
        int display()

        double display()

        Only return type changed.


    Compiler Error.

    Compile-Time Polymorphism, Also called Static Binding, Early Binding

    Method Overloading

    Compiler decides

    Which method should execute

    During compilation.

